WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:02.000
Hiya.

2
00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.620
If you're already using Claude Code, Codex, or one of the other

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.580
frontier coding agents seriously, then you probably don't need me to

4
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.160
tell you how good they've become, right? They're amazing.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:14.080
We

6
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.700
were already using them pretty heavily ourselves, and the amount of

7
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.920
coding work a person could suddenly take on

8
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:24.160
changed dramatically.

9
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.500
But something else became pretty obvious to us as well.

10
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.060
The faster these models got, the more work we could throw at them, sure,

11
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.340
and then the harder it became to keep everything around that work under control.

12
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.460
Not just the code, the context, the decisions, dependencies,

13
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.840
the tests, the tools. What had actually happened?

14
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.280
What still needed to be checked? What had we agreed

15
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.600
on earlier, and what was safe to ship?

16
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.160
And that led us to a fairly simple question.

17
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.480
The agents were getting incredibly capable, but they were still

18
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.000
largely working inside environments that had been designed for people.

19
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.401
So what would happen

20
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:07.800
if we built the environment

21
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.540
around the AI itself?

22
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.960
That's really where GrapeVibe came from.

23
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.480
We built it for ourselves, quite literally we built it for ourselves.

24
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.300
We weren't sitting around trying to think of an AI product to launch.

25
00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.300
We were trying to figure out how to work differently.

26
00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:29.640
We wanted to give frontier AI much more of the engineering work

27
00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.520
without losing visibility, and without losing our judgment or

28
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.960
control as the work grew and got bigger.

29
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.640
And I reckon that distinction really matters,

30
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.080
because I don't think

31
00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.940
the real problem with AI coding is simply whether the model writes good

32
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.960
code, because sometimes it does. Sometimes it gets something

33
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.180
almost right. Sometimes it gets something wrong in a

34
00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.180
way that's completely obvious, and sometimes it gets something wrong in a way

35
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.100
that isn't obvious at all. But that's always been true, right?

36
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.560
In one form or another, with software engineering, because

37
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.720
people make mistakes too. So the important question

38
00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.860
is what happens around the work? Can you see what

39
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.320
changed? Can you see why it changed?

40
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.500
Did the tests actually run? What passed? What failed?

41
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.640
What assumptions were made? Does the agent understand the

42
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.500
rest of the system?

43
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.360
Can you change direction while it's working?

44
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:33.940
Can another piece of work pick up where this one stopped and

45
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.180
without starting from scratch again?

46
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.840
Can you actually stand behind the result?

47
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.520
And that's what we mean when we talk about production discipline.

48
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.800
GrapeVibe isn't asking you to blindly trust AI.

49
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.400
In fact, we're doing the opposite.

50
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.700
We think the way you give AI more responsibility

51
00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:57.520
is by building an engineering system around it that gives you

52
00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.920
more visibility into what it's doing.

53
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.980
Your intent starts to work. The agent can inspect the code,

54
00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.120
work with files, use tools, run tests, make changes,

55
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.640
and basically carry the work forward.

56
00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.280
But the context stays with the work.

57
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.420
The evidence stays with the work. The decisions stay connected to the

58
00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.480
work. And ultimately, it's your judgment that decides what gets

59
00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.460
shipped. That's a very different idea from just generating more

60
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.400
code. Code generation is already becoming

61
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:30.660
cheap.

62
00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.080
In a few years, it's going to be ridiculously cheap.

63
00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.140
So the interesting question to me becomes how much

64
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.540
real engineering can one capable person take responsibility for?

65
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.660
Not how many lines of code can they generate.

66
00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:50.760
How much can they actually take from an idea through

67
00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:54.700
implementation, testing, iteration, and then into production?

68
00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.700
That's the thing we actually care about, and it's also why

69
00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.760
GrapeVibe is designed around whichever frontier models you

70
00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.960
choose. We're not trying to replace Claude or Codex.

71
00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:10.440
In fact, we actually want them to become way better than they

72
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.240
are now. Because if the models become twice as capable,

73
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.481
we think you should be able to take on a whole lot more.

74
00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.620
If they become 10 times as capable, the surrounding engineering

75
00:04:21.721 --> 00:04:25.620
system needs to let you take advantage of that

76
00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.640
rather than simply creating 10 times more stuff for someone to review.

77
00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.040
That's the bet we've made behind GrapeVibe.

78
00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.780
Better models should translate into

79
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.100
more engineering reaching production,

80
00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.320
not just more code being produced.

81
00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.940
And as the work gets bigger, GrapeVibe can grow around it.

82
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.420
You can connect the tools you already use.

83
00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.920
You can work across your code, your data, your systems.

84
00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.020
You can run different projects on different nodes.

85
00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.740
Those nodes can

86
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.140
work together. You can give agents access to what they need

87
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.320
without simply handing them everything.

88
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.360
You can keep context close to the work,

89
00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.800
and you can inspect what happened rather than just hoping it worked.

90
00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.300
We're still building it.

91
00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:18.260
There are

92
00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.300
most likely things we'll want to improve.

93
00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.880
There are things in here that probably won't look or work exactly the

94
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.840
way you're used to. And that's partly because we've tried

95
00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.660
to solve a slightly different problem.

96
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.500
If what you want is an AI autocomplete

97
00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.680
or another coding assistant,

98
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.900
there are already brilliant products out there for that.

99
00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.480
We built GrapeVibe for people who want to put frontier

100
00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:51.600
AI to work across real engineering and remain in control of the

101
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.460
outcome. People who look at what Claude and Codex can

102
00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.380
already do and think, "I should be able to take on more than I could

103
00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.540
before." That's how we think as well.

104
00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:06.040
Not only that, but that's how we've learned to work every

105
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.080
day. And GrapeVibe is the system we built around that way of

106
00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.280
working. So

107
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:13.040
if this

108
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:17.840
sounds like the way you'd like to work or want

109
00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:18.320
to work,

110
00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.120
give it a go. Give GrapeVibe a proper go.

111
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.400
You get 14 days. That's more than enough time to put

112
00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.480
something real through it. And I mean

113
00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:33.180
real. Don't give it a demo task. Give it a piece of work

114
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.160
you actually care about. Something with enough complexity that

115
00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:40.140
you can see whether this changes what you're able to take on.

116
00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:43.640
If it's not for you, no stress, just cancel it.

117
00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.140
But we think that if you're already working seriously with

118
00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.620
frontier AI, you'll understand pretty quickly why we built

119
00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.220
GrapeVibe and why we now work this way ourselves.

120
00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:59.680
Again, we built it for ourselves, literally, and

121
00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:03.340
now we want to see what other people like us can do with it.

122
00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.440
Thanks so much for your time.
